  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Thursday: A strong upper ridge centered over \nthe south central US combined with a low off the California \ncoast is providing a broad south to southwest flow across the \nwestern US. The south to southwest flow will bring continued \nwarming of temperatures on Wednesday, with hot weather \npersisting into Thursday. Continued light north to northeast \nwinds into Wednesday morning will allow for continued haze and \nsmoke to linger with the highest amounts across Central and \nNortheast Washington given closer proximity to active wildfires.\nThere is not much in the way of wind given the influence of \nhigh pressure through Thursday morning so not expecting any \nmajor improvements to the smoke and haze situation through then.\n\nThe main weather feature of interest is the low off the\nCalifornia coast which will send an increase in monsoonal\nmoisture up into the region along with a mid level short wave\npassage on Thursday. Precipitable water values increase to\n150-180 percent of normal while 700-500mb most unstable CAPE, as\nwell as surface based CAPE Thursday afternoon increase to as\nmuch as 300-700 J/KG. The one missing ingredient is strong mid\nlevel forcing. With the low still well off the coast and the\nbest upper level diffluence to the south across Oregon, the\nforecast is leaning towards isolated coverage of thunderstorms,\nwith the best chances along the East Slopes of the Cascades into\nthe northern mountains, as well as over SE Washington into the\nCentral Panhandle where models indicate the two areas of best\ninstability. Yet would not rule out thunderstorms for any areas.\nAnd even isolated thunderstorms are a concern given the hot and\ndry weather preceding Thursday and dry fuels. Despite the high \nmoisture content in the atmosphere, the quick movement of storms\nis expected to produce only light rain with storms, further \nraising concerns for new fire starts.\n\nFriday and Saturday: On Friday a large low from Alaska begins to\nswing into British Columbia, which will act as a kicker to bring\nthe offshore low into the region as an open wave on Saturday.\nThis transition will bring dry and breezy winds on Friday,\nfollowed by an additional wind increase for Saturday as a cold\nfront moves through. This will result in elevated to critical\nfire weather conditions as west to southwest winds increase to\n15 to 25 mph with gusts of 30-40 MPH. A cooling trend will \nbegin to help increase the relative humidity, but yet the \nincreasing winds will be a concern for any new or going fires.\nNo precipitation is expected as this system moves in, except for\nthe Cascade crest with most members from the ECMWF generating at\nleast light precipitation for Stevens Pass.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: Another low associated with the upper\nlevel trough over British Columbia intensifies off the central\nBC coast on Sunday, potentially sending another weak front\nacross the region on Monday. This will help keep our\ntemperatures in check with highs down in the 80s, which is near\nto slightly cooler than normal. This should help bring some\nmoderation to the fire weather conditions for early next week.\nYet little to no precipitation is expected with this system\nexcept for a chance of light showers along the Cascade crest. JW",
